Auto Ticket Classification using Flow Designer
7.Project Documentation Phase

7.1 Project Documentation Contents
The final project documentation should contain:

Project title.
Auto Ticket Classification Using Flow Designer
Introduction.
The Auto Ticket Classification using Flow Designer project is designed to automatically classify IT support tickets based on their issue 
description. The system identifies the appropriate Category and Subcategory for each ticket without requiring manual classification.
The project uses ServiceNow Flow Designer to automate ticket processing and improve the efficiency of IT support operations.

Problem statement.
In a manual ticket management system, support staff need to read each ticket and manually select its category and subcategory. 
This process can take more time and may result in incorrect classification.
The proposed system automatically analyzes the issue description and assigns the appropriate category and subcategory. 
It also helps in sending automated notifications and maintaining ticket information in a structured format.

Existing system.
In the existing system:
Tickets are classified manually.
Support staff need to select the category and subcategory.
Manual classification takes additional time.
Incorrect classification may occur.
Ticket processing can be inconsistent.
Notifications may require manual action.

Proposed system.
The proposed system uses ServiceNow Flow Designer to automate ticket classification.
When a ticket is created, the system checks the issue description and applies predefined classification rules.
Based on the matching conditions, the system automatically assigns the appropriate category and subcategory.
The system can also send an automated email notification to the caller after ticket creation.

Objectives.

The main objectives of this project are:
To automatically classify IT tickets.
To assign suitable categories and subcategories.
To reduce manual intervention.
To improve ticket processing speed.
To provide consistent ticket classification.
To send automated notifications to users.
To make the workflow easy to maintain and scale.

Requirements.
Functional Requirements
The system should accept IT support tickets.
The system should read the issue description.
The system should identify the appropriate category.
The system should assign the corresponding subcategory.
The system should update the ticket automatically.
The system should send an automated notification to the caller.

System design.
The system consists of the following major steps:
Ticket Creation → Read Issue Description → Apply Classification Rules → Assign Category → Assign Subcategory → Update Ticket 
→ Send Notification
The workflow is designed to process tickets automatically with minimum manual intervention.

Flow Designer workflow.
The Flow Designer workflow automates the ticket classification process.
Workflow Steps:
A new IT ticket is created.
The Flow Designer flow is triggered.
The system reads the issue description.
Conditions are checked based on the issue.
The appropriate category is identified.
The corresponding subcategory is selected.
The ticket information is updated.
An automated email notification is sent to the caller.
This workflow reduces manual work and provides faster ticket processing.

Classification rules.
Classification rules are used to identify the correct category and subcategory based on the ticket description.
For example:
Hardware-related issue → Hardware category
Software-related issue → Software category
Network-related issue → Network category
Password/Login issue → Access-related category
The exact category and subcategory are selected according to the conditions defined in the Flow Designer.

Implementation details.
The project is implemented using ServiceNow Flow Designer.
The flow contains trigger conditions, classification logic, record update actions, and notification actions.
The conditions examine the ticket information and determine the appropriate classification.
After classification, the ticket record is automatically updated with the selected category and subcategory.

